A||N||12||87ECO5||Flickering lights (both interior and exterior)||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||04-30-2001||08:30 AM||||I ran several searches to get some answers on this ("flickering lights", "lights bright dim", "flashing lights", etc.) but didn't find what I was looking for.  If you are aware of threads that cover this, please point me to them.<P>I've got an '87 economy coupe with a recently transplanted 3.4 V6.  The lights (both interior and exterior) go ... bright ... dim ... bright ... dim ... bright ... dim ... at a fairly rapid pace.  The pace of the flickering is constant - it does not vary with engine speed.<P>The alternator belt is new (and recently tightened).  I don't know how old the alternator is.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated.  TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||04-30-2001||08:55 AM||||Does this happen with no accessories or anything else on besides the lights being in use?<BR>Did the lights do this before the engine swap?<BR>If not, I would suspect a bad or not hooked up ground wire in the engine compartment.<BR>See if you can find a heavy ground wire and run it from the engine block to the chassis and see if there are any results.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||04-30-2001||09:01 AM||||My 84 kinda does the same thing. When I click on the turn signal or step on the brake  every instrument and light in the car pulses, kinda ticks me off||||reg||5
Z||000003||87ECO5||04-30-2001||09:27 AM||||During the drive to work this morning, all I had on was the headlights.  They (and the gauge lights) flickered the entire way to work.<P>Once in the parking lot, with the headlights off and the door open, the dome lights did the same thing.  Also, the voltmeter on the aux. gauge cluster bounced just slightly at the same pace as the flickering.<P>It did not do this before the engine swap.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1988LRC||04-30-2001||09:30 AM||||bad ground||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||04-30-2001||01:22 PM||||Seems strange that a bad ground would result in such regular flickering.  Wonder if the alternator you got in the swap has a tired voltage regulator?  What kind of voltage are you running?<P>Of course, my electrical problem-solving track record is shaky at best.  Sounds like a job for WTIII, Boilermaker and/or fiero56 to me.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ray b||04-30-2001||02:07 PM||||check wires at rear bulkhead firewall passthru chaffing can cause shorts and or fire. my 87 se burned up in jan 2001 because of this elct short.tape or replace wires itis cheaper than fire damage.<P>question wonder and stay wierd||||reg||1
Z||000007||My7Fieros||04-30-2001||02:37 PM||||Same thing happened to me on my 86 GT.....its a bad voltage reg in the alt.<BR>Replace alt. M7F||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||04-30-2001||03:01 PM||||Although its not the easiest job in the world, I've got a spare alternator for v-6 if you want to try it out. Of course, AutoZone will check them for free.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||04-30-2001||08:04 PM||||Unplug the AC compressor to make certain it is off. If that stops it, then you likely have problems with ground straps or the alternator. Have the alt and battery load tested just to make sure.||||reg||1
Z||000010||JR||04-30-2001||08:22 PM||||Guaranteed it's the alternator, as I just changed mine 3 weeks ago and voila---no more flicker.  My mechanic advises that there are 3 rectifiers in the alternator and when one goes bad the alternator will still function.  And yes my high frequency flicker was evident for the last 3 years before the alternator finally gave out.  Don't waste your time looking for a bad ground as I did!!<BR>Bad ground means you'll have intermittent interruptions in what ever accessory you have turned on, not a 10 or 20 cycle per second consistent flicker that we are talking about here.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
Z||000011||william_thorniii||05-01-2001||01:18 PM||||Friends,<P>I went up and checked out the Plastic Fantastic last night.  The things I discovered were as follows:  The problem only occurs while the cars energy is supplied by the alternator, I checked the voltage and ampere output of the alternator and according to my inexpensive multimeter they both appeared relatively constant and every device in the vehicle experiences these surges.  My deductions from this information is 1) that the alternator is likely the culpret, 2) that the variations must be extremely slight (hardly noticeable to the eye) 3) that is not related to a ground since every device is affected & 4) that my multimeter sucks.  Do these conclusions seem reasonible?  If so, please let me know.  <P>I intend on checking the amp variation at one of the devices and comparing it with the variation seen at the alternator for comparison.  I would assume that amps would be a better basis for judgement as they have a better resolution due to their smaller unit size is this true?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000012||84Bill||05-01-2001||01:40 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>Friends,<P>Do these conclusions seem reasonible?  If so, please let me know.  <BR>William T. Thorn, III</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>William <BR>Your conclusions do seem reasonable enough<BR>I have conducted my own test with a 1 84 Fiero and a control subject. I will post my results within the next few hours.<P>William H Stewart III<P>krap sorry lost the info I was going to post<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-01-2001).]||||reg||3
